Let $k$ be a field and let $G$ be an affine algebraic group over $k$ (an affine group scheme of finite type over $k$, [[def-affine-scheme]], [[def-group-scheme-over-a-field]]). The following are equivalent:

(a) $G$ is unipotent ([[def-unipotent-algebraic-group]]);

(b) $G$ is isomorphic to a closed subgroup scheme of the upper unitriangular group scheme $U_n$ for some $n\ge1$ ([[def-upper-unitriangular-group-scheme]]);

(c) the coordinate Hopf algebra $O(G)$ is coconnected ([[def-coconnected-hopf-algebra]]).

Equivalently, $G$ admits a faithful finite-dimensional rational representation that is unipotent. No smoothness, connectedness or perfectness is assumed; in positive characteristic the infinitesimal group $\alpha_p$ and the constant group $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)_k$ are unipotent groups that are non-smooth, respectively non-connected.

